<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Totila</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/552">552</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Baduila</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Totila, also called Baduila, was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> from 541 to 552. He revived the Gothic cause after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>' first conquest, retaking most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, capturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> twice and freeing slaves and tenants to join his army. He was mortally wounded when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> defeated him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-taginae">Battle of Taginae</a> in 552.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/totila.jpg" alt="Totila" width="320" height="151" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 6th-century · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tremissis_of_Baduila.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Totila was the name used by the Byzantine historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>, who accompanied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothic-war-535-554">Gothic War</a> and is the main source for his life. His real name was Baduila, as his coinage shows. Marco Cristini has argued that the consistent use of Totila by Eastern Roman authors may have served a propagandistic purpose, encouraged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> to delegitimise him as a usurper. He was born in Treviso, a grandnephew of Theudis, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, and a sword-bearer.</p><p>The Ostrogothic nobles elected him king in the autumn of 541, after the killing of Ildibad and then of Eraric, both slain by the Gothic nobility for attempting to surrender to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. The official Byzantine position, adopted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> and by the Romanized Goth Jordanes, was that he was a usurper, but according to Peter Heather he came from a prominent Gothic family.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 542 Totila defended Verona and defeated a Roman army at the Battle of Faventia, then won at Mucellium, captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> in the spring of 543 on generous terms, and by 543 had retaken most of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. He won support from the lower classes by liberating slaves and distributing land to the peasants, though Cristini holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> exaggerated the slaves' part in his army. His strategy was to move fast and take the countryside, leaving Byzantine forces in well-defended cities and ports, and he collected rent and taxes instead of plundering. Procopius also records cruelties, including the beheading of Herculanus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Perugia, and the maiming of the captive general Demetrius.</p><p>Totila besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from December 545 and entered the practically empty city a year later, praying at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica</a>. Belisarius dissuaded him from razing it, and retook it by April 546. After Belisarius retreated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 549, Totila recaptured Rome and went on to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. A Byzantine fleet defeated his own in 551.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 552 the eunuch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> led a force including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a> and Heruls against him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-taginae">Battle of Taginae</a>. Ordered to charge with spears alone, the Gothic cavalry rode into the crossfire of Narses's archers and broke. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> reports that a Gepid officer, Asbados, speared the fleeing king in the back, and that Totila died at a village called Caprae. A second account says a stray arrow struck him as he fled.</p><p>His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teia">Teia</a> died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mons-lactarius">Battle of Mons Lactarius</a>. The Pragmatic Sanction of 554 voided Totila's edicts and called him a most abominable tyrant. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was so ravaged by the war that normal life could not return, and in 568 most of the country was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alboin">Alboin</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Totila” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After a long siege, the Ostrogothic king <b>Totila</b> entered Rome when Isaurian guards opened a gate.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-17">17 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Totila</b> was killed at Busta Gallorum in 552, and Teia at Mons Lactarius in 552 or 553.</blockquote><a href="/state/ostrogothic-kingdom">Ostrogothic Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Treviso, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Gualdo Tadino, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.67,12.25&amp;year=542" aria-label="Treviso on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4840 554 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Totila"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5290 824h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5290 824h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.67,12.25&amp;year=542">What was here in 542 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
